{
  "query": "An online retailer that runs limited-time sales uses Memorystore for Redis to track active shopping sessions and real-time top seller rankings. The team needs strong availability and a disaster recovery posture while keeping read and write latency very low for users concentrated in one region. Which deployment approach should they implement?",
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Memorystore for Redis Basic Tier with persistence and scheduled exports to Cloud Storage",
      "explanation": "This approach relies on data persistence for recovery but uses a single node and does not provide automatic failover",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Memorystore for Redis with a primary in one region and a replica in a distant region",
      "explanation": "This deployment places nodes across regions which adds significant network latency due to long-distance traffic",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Memorystore for Redis Standard Tier with a primary and replica in different zones within one region",
      "explanation": "This configuration uses zonal redundancy in a single region and supports automatic failover while keeping latency low",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Memorystore for Redis with both the primary and replica in the same zone",
      "explanation": "This keeps traffic local but concentrates both nodes in one failure domain which reduces resilience",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "answer": "<p>The correct choice is <b>Memorystore for Redis Standard Tier with a primary and replica in different zones within one region</b>.</p><p>This approach delivers high availability because the replica in a separate zone can automatically take over if the primary fails. It maintains very low latency since all traffic stays within a single region where the users are concentrated. It strengthens disaster recovery posture for zonal failures without introducing cross region latency.</p><p><i>Memorystore for Redis Basic Tier with persistence and scheduled exports to Cloud Storage</i> is not appropriate because Basic Tier is a single node and does not provide high availability. Exports improve recoverability but they are not real time and require manual restore steps so they do not meet the strong availability requirement.</p><p><i>Memorystore for Redis with a primary in one region and a replica in a distant region</i> is not supported for Memorystore for Redis and would also add significant latency for writes and failover if it were possible. This does not satisfy the need for very low latency for a regionally concentrated user base.</p><p><i>Memorystore for Redis with both the primary and replica in the same zone</i> does not protect against zonal outages and therefore fails to provide strong availability or a meaningful disaster recovery posture.</p>",
